WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.717
To traverse a tree is to visit

00:00:02.798 --> 00:00:04.731
every node exactly once.

00:00:04.812 --> 00:00:07.068
Visit means whatever we need

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:09.405
to do with a node: print it,

00:00:09.405 --> 00:00:11.741
add it to a sum, or copy it.

00:00:11.741 --> 00:00:14.158
A list has one natural order.

00:00:14.158 --> 00:00:17.220
A binary tree has three classic ones.

00:00:17.570 --> 00:00:19.746
Preorder: visit the node,

00:00:19.746 --> 00:00:22.532
then traverse the left subtree,

00:00:22.532 --> 00:00:26.189
then the right subtree, each in preorder.

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:29.062
Inorder: the left subtree first,

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:32.457
then the node, then the right subtree.

00:00:32.457 --> 00:00:34.982
Postorder: the left subtree,

00:00:34.982 --> 00:00:38.290
the right subtree, and the node last.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:39.604
In all three,

00:00:39.679 --> 00:00:42.275
the left subtree is finished before

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:44.204
the right subtree starts.

00:00:44.204 --> 00:00:45.762
Only the root moves.

00:00:45.837 --> 00:00:48.433
All three follow one branch as deep

00:00:48.507 --> 00:00:50.659
as it goes before backing up,

00:00:50.659 --> 00:00:53.701
so they are forms of depth-first search

00:00:53.701 --> 00:00:56.520
which comes back in lecture 19.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.585
Let's expand preorder by hand on this tree.

00:01:00.585 --> 00:01:02.611
The root comes first: A,

00:01:02.692 --> 00:01:04.961
followed by A's left subtree

00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:06.825
and A's right subtree,

00:01:06.825 --> 00:01:08.689
each still to be done.

00:01:08.689 --> 00:01:10.715
Expand the leftmost one.

00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:13.389
A's left subtree is rooted at B,

00:01:13.389 --> 00:01:16.631
so B comes next, then B's left subtree.

00:01:16.631 --> 00:01:18.900
B's right subtree is empty.

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:22.388
B's left subtree is just D, a leaf.

00:01:22.388 --> 00:01:26.064
Now everything left of the root is done.

00:01:26.064 --> 00:01:29.022
A's right subtree starts with C,

00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:32.250
followed by C's left and C's right.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.634
C's left subtree is rooted at E.

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.435
E's left is empty,

00:01:37.435 --> 00:01:41.132
so after E comes its right subtree: G.

00:01:41.132 --> 00:01:44.640
Then C's right subtree, rooted at F,

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:48.343
and F's two leaves, H and I.

00:01:48.343 --> 00:01:53.492
So the preorder is A, B, D, C, E, G, F, H,

00:01:53.492 --> 00:01:53.851
I.

00:01:53.851 --> 00:01:57.323
The root always comes first,

00:01:57.443 --> 00:02:01.035
and a node always comes before

00:02:01.155 --> 00:02:03.550
everything below it.

00:02:04.130 --> 00:02:06.761
In Java, the three methods are tiny.

00:02:06.761 --> 00:02:07.212
Here,

00:02:07.212 --> 00:02:10.370
visit means adding the element to a list.

00:02:10.370 --> 00:02:13.152
If x is null, the call does nothing;

00:02:13.227 --> 00:02:15.257
the empty tree is traversed

00:02:15.332 --> 00:02:16.610
by doing nothing.

00:02:16.960 --> 00:02:19.355
The first call is on 8

00:02:19.355 --> 00:02:21.661
which is visited at once.

00:02:21.661 --> 00:02:25.386
Then it calls itself on its left subtree

00:02:25.386 --> 00:02:26.362
and waits.

00:02:26.362 --> 00:02:29.200
The call on 3 visits 3

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.537
and calls on 2, which is visited.

00:02:32.537 --> 00:02:34.757
2's subtrees are empty

00:02:34.757 --> 00:02:38.256
so those two calls return straight away.

00:02:38.256 --> 00:02:41.670
Back in 3, the right subtree: 5.

00:02:42.020 --> 00:02:45.405
3 is finished, and we are back in 8

00:02:45.405 --> 00:02:48.020
which calls on its right subtree.

00:02:48.020 --> 00:02:49.405
7 is visited.

00:02:49.405 --> 00:02:51.482
Its left subtree is empty

00:02:51.482 --> 00:02:53.020
so next comes 9.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.847
Every call returns

00:02:55.847 --> 00:03:01.453
and the visited list is 8, 3, 2

00:03:01.453 --> 00:03:03.930
5, 7, 9.

00:03:04.510 --> 00:03:07.629
Inorder is the same method with the visit

00:03:07.705 --> 00:03:10.215
line moved between the two calls.

00:03:10.215 --> 00:03:13.030
Postorder moves it after both calls.

00:03:13.380 --> 00:03:14.735
Nothing else changes.

00:03:14.799 --> 00:03:17.121
That is the whole difference between

00:03:17.186 --> 00:03:18.540
the three traversals.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:19.945
Now predict.

00:03:19.945 --> 00:03:22.077
Before the program shows them

00:03:22.077 --> 00:03:23.796
write down the preorder

00:03:23.796 --> 00:03:26.821
the inorder and the postorder of this tree.

00:03:26.821 --> 00:03:29.434
In which order does 8 come first

00:03:29.434 --> 00:03:31.497
in which is it in the middle

00:03:31.497 --> 00:03:33.216
and in which is it last?

00:03:33.216 --> 00:03:35.760
Pause the video and write them down.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.978
Here is the output.

00:03:41.978 --> 00:03:47.808
Preorder: 8, 3, 2, 5, 7

00:03:47.808 --> 00:03:48.641
9.

00:03:48.641 --> 00:03:51.695
The root comes first.

00:03:51.695 --> 00:03:57.387
Inorder: 2, 3, 5, 8, 7

00:03:57.387 --> 00:03:58.220
9.

00:03:58.570 --> 00:03:59.633
In inorder

00:03:59.633 --> 00:04:03.980
8 sits exactly between its left subtree

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.941
2, 3, 5, and its right subtree

00:04:07.941 --> 00:04:09.196
7, 9.

00:04:09.196 --> 00:04:13.254
Postorder: 2, 5, 3, 9, 7

00:04:13.254 --> 00:04:13.930
8.

00:04:14.280 --> 00:04:16.838
The root is first in preorder

00:04:16.838 --> 00:04:18.458
last in postorder

00:04:18.458 --> 00:04:22.124
and in inorder it splits the two subtrees.

00:04:22.210 --> 00:04:24.256
That last fact is how we

00:04:24.341 --> 00:04:27.240
will rebuild a tree, in part four.

00:04:27.820 --> 00:04:32.079
A warning: inorder is not sorted order.

00:04:32.079 --> 00:04:36.448
The inorder of this tree is 2, 3

00:04:36.448 --> 00:04:39.287
5, 8, 7, 9.

00:04:39.287 --> 00:04:42.127
8 comes before 7

00:04:42.127 --> 00:04:44.420
so it is not sorted.

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:47.568
This tree is not a binary search tree.

00:04:47.568 --> 00:04:49.629
Next lecture adds the rule

00:04:49.629 --> 00:04:52.427
everything left of a node is smaller

00:04:52.427 --> 00:04:55.225
and everything right of it is larger.

00:04:55.225 --> 00:04:58.170
Only then does inorder come out sorted.

00:04:58.750 --> 00:05:01.537
You have already met two traversals

00:05:01.617 --> 00:05:03.209
without their names.

00:05:03.209 --> 00:05:04.245
In part one

00:05:04.324 --> 00:05:07.111
the program TreeTerms printed every

00:05:07.191 --> 00:05:09.580
node with its method describe.

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:11.650
It prints the node first

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.278
then calls itself on each child.

00:05:14.278 --> 00:05:16.747
Node first, then the subtrees

00:05:16.747 --> 00:05:18.180
that is preorder.

00:05:18.180 --> 00:05:21.365
That is why it printed Organism, Plant

00:05:21.365 --> 00:05:24.630
Animal, Mammal, Fish, from the top down.

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:27.587
The height of a binary tree needs the

00:05:27.657 --> 00:05:30.335
heights of both subtrees before it can

00:05:30.405 --> 00:05:32.378
say anything about the node.

00:05:32.378 --> 00:05:34.280
Subtrees first, node last

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:35.619
that is postorder.

00:05:35.619 --> 00:05:37.380
Size works the same way.

00:05:37.730 --> 00:05:39.798
And inorder will come into

00:05:39.878 --> 00:05:41.548
its own next lecture.

00:05:41.548 --> 00:05:43.855
Keep these patterns in mind

00:05:43.855 --> 00:05:46.878
when a node needs results from below

00:05:46.878 --> 00:05:48.231
think postorder.

00:05:48.231 --> 00:05:50.617
When it hands something down

00:05:50.617 --> 00:05:51.890
think preorder.

00:05:52.470 --> 00:05:54.856
How long does a traversal take?

00:05:54.856 --> 00:05:57.627
Every node is visited exactly once

00:05:57.704 --> 00:06:00.705
and each call does a constant amount of

00:06:00.782 --> 00:06:03.630
work besides its two recursive calls.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:05.420
More carefully

00:06:05.420 --> 00:06:08.589
if the left subtree has k nodes

00:06:08.589 --> 00:06:12.335
the right one has n − k − 1

00:06:12.335 --> 00:06:15.215
and T(n) is at most T(k)

00:06:15.215 --> 00:06:18.192
plus T(n − k − 1)

00:06:18.192 --> 00:06:19.825
plus a constant.

00:06:19.921 --> 00:06:22.321
Induction gives T(n) at

00:06:22.418 --> 00:06:24.722
most a constant times n

00:06:24.722 --> 00:06:28.467
and every node must be visited anyway

00:06:28.467 --> 00:06:31.060
so the time is Θ(n).

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:34.001
You can also count the calls

00:06:34.001 --> 00:06:35.788
one for every node

00:06:35.788 --> 00:06:39.451
and one for every empty subtree reached.

00:06:39.541 --> 00:06:41.864
A binary tree with n nodes

00:06:41.953 --> 00:06:43.919
has 2n child links

00:06:43.919 --> 00:06:47.225
n − 1 of which point to nodes

00:06:47.225 --> 00:06:49.369
so n + 1 are null.

00:06:49.369 --> 00:06:52.050
In all, 2n + 1 calls

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:54.730
13 for these six nodes.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:58.686
The program CountCalls confirms it

00:06:58.686 --> 00:07:01.290
six nodes, 13 calls.

00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:03.990
Nine nodes, 19 calls.

00:07:04.340 --> 00:07:07.051
The extra space is the call stack.

00:07:07.051 --> 00:07:08.247
At any moment

00:07:08.327 --> 00:07:10.799
the waiting calls are the nodes

00:07:10.879 --> 00:07:13.749
on one path from the root, plus one

00:07:13.749 --> 00:07:14.706
here 8

00:07:14.786 --> 00:07:18.055
3 and 2 are waiting while the empty

00:07:18.135 --> 00:07:20.527
left subtree of 2 is called.

00:07:20.527 --> 00:07:23.796
So the stack is at most h + 2 deep

00:07:23.796 --> 00:07:26.348
O(log n) for a complete tree

00:07:26.348 --> 00:07:28.660
but O(n) for a skewed one.

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:30.012
To sum up.

00:07:30.012 --> 00:07:32.403
One: preorder visits the node

00:07:32.403 --> 00:07:35.026
then the left and right subtrees.

00:07:35.026 --> 00:07:37.880
Inorder puts the node in the middle

00:07:37.880 --> 00:07:40.040
and postorder puts it last.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:41.506
Two: in all three

00:07:41.583 --> 00:07:44.283
the left subtree is finished before

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:47.600
the right one starts; only the root moves.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:51.822
Three: each traversal takes Θ(n) time

00:07:51.822 --> 00:07:54.198
with 2n + 1 calls

00:07:54.198 --> 00:07:58.070
and a call stack about as deep as the tree.

00:07:58.420 --> 00:07:59.091
Try this.

00:07:59.091 --> 00:08:00.955
Write down the preorder

00:08:00.955 --> 00:08:04.011
inorder and postorder of the tree 3

00:08:04.011 --> 00:08:06.322
with children 9 and 20

00:08:06.322 --> 00:08:09.676
where 20 has children 15 and 7.

00:08:09.751 --> 00:08:11.540
Then check them with the

00:08:11.615 --> 00:08:13.180
program in the notes.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:16.035
In part four: expression trees

00:08:16.035 --> 00:08:19.672
postfix notation, and rebuilding a tree from

00:08:19.752 --> 00:08:21.530
two of its traversals.

